Pull the image on the nodes of the object not running the container, so a
failover finds it in the store of the engine, and neither waits for the
registry nor fails with it.

If not set, the `node.image_prefetch` keyword of the node decides, `true`
by default.

The image prefetched by a node is the `image` keyword as this node
evaluates it, the one its start would run. It is pulled when the store of
the engine does not hold it, and never pulled again once there: a prefetch
does not refresh a moving tag as `latest`, which the nodes may then hold
different builds of. Pin a tag that does not move, or a digest, for the
nodes to run the same build.

The prefetch is a best effort, done by the daemon when the configuration
of the object changes. A pull that failed is tried again later, less and
less often, and the resource status warns of the missing image meanwhile.
An image removed from the store, as by an `image prune`, is pulled again,
except from the store of a `rootless_user`, which the daemon does not
watch: that one is pulled again on the next change of the configuration.

A start is not changed by the prefetch: it pulls a missing image, and
`image_pull_policy = always` still pulls at every start.
